summary_en.experiments:
The experiments cover LVLMs from 2B to 8B parameters on multimodal reasoning benchmarks. The headline claim is that PTD-PO consistently outperforms plain RLVR and distillation-based baselines, while maintaining higher policy entropy, improving recovery from failed rollouts, and reducing the memory overhead of online distillation.

The most important evidence to inspect is not just average benchmark accuracy, but the trade-off the method is supposed to improve:
1. whether gains are consistent across model scales;
2. whether PTD-PO beats external-teacher OPD and answer-conditioned self-distillation in the joint space of performance, training cost, and entropy preservation;
3. whether Top-K JSD with tail compensation actually saves memory and remains stable relative to full-vocabulary or naive top-k distillation;
4. whether failed-rollout recovery analyses show real credit-assignment benefits rather than generic regularization.

The abstract does not provide concrete benchmark numbers, so the main tables matter. When reading, pay special attention to which open LVLM backbones are used, how large the gains are on each benchmark, whether entropy-collapse curves are visibly delayed, and how the hint ablations behave. The hint ablations are especially important: visual cues only, textual reasoning hints only, and both together. Those details determine how convincing the method really is.
